__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOKS_1",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{source:{id:'atomic-habits',title:'Atomic Habits',author:'James Clear',type:'book',year:2018,topics:['productivity','psychology'],emoji:'\ud83e\uddf1',description:'How tiny changes, repeated daily, compound into remarkable results.'},ideas:[{topic:'productivity',title:'Get 1% better every day',body:'Small improvements feel meaningless in the moment, but they compound. Getting 1% better each day for a year makes you roughly 37 times better. Getting 1% worse each day takes you close to zero. Your habits are the interest rate on your life, so judge them by where they lead, not by how they feel today.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'Systems beat goals',body:'Winners and losers often have the same goals. What separates them is the system: the daily process that produces results. A goal is a moment in time. A system is what you do every day. Fall in love with the process, and the results tend to take care of themselves.'},{topic:'psychology',title:'Identity-based habits',body:'The strongest habits come from who you believe you are. Instead of "I want to run a marathon", think "I am a runner". Every action you take is a vote for the type of person you want to become. You do not need a unanimous vote, just a majority.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'The four laws of behavior change',body:'To build a good habit: make it obvious (clear cues), make it attractive (pair it with something you enjoy), make it easy (reduce friction), and make it satisfying (reward yourself right away). To break a bad habit, invert each law: make it invisible, unattractive, difficult and unsatisfying.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'The two-minute rule',body:'When starting a new habit, scale it down until it takes less than two minutes. "Read before bed" becomes "read one page". "Do yoga" becomes "roll out the mat". A habit must be established before it can be improved. Master the art of showing up first.'}]},{source:{id:'thinking-fast-slow',title:'Thinking, Fast and Slow',author:'Daniel Kahneman',type:'book',year:2011,topics:['psychology'],emoji:'\ud83d\udc22',description:'A Nobel laureate maps the two systems that drive how we think and decide.'},ideas:[{topic:'psychology',title:'System 1 and System 2',body:'Your mind runs two modes. System 1 is fast, automatic and intuitive: it reads faces and finishes "bread and...". System 2 is slow, effortful and logical: it does long division. System 1 runs the show most of the time, and it is quick but prone to predictable errors.'},{topic:'psychology',title:'What you see is all there is',body:'Your brain builds the most coherent story it can from the information in front of it, and rarely asks what is missing. That is why a one-sided argument can feel convincing. Before deciding, ask yourself: what would I need to know that I don\'t know yet?'},{topic:'psychology',title:'Anchoring',body:'An arbitrary number you saw recently can pull your estimates toward it, even when you know it is irrelevant. First prices in a negotiation, "was $199" tags and suggested tip amounts all work this way. When estimating, form your own number before looking at anyone else\'s.'},{topic:'money',title:'Losses hurt twice as much',body:'Losing $100 feels roughly twice as bad as winning $100 feels good. This loss aversion makes us hold losing investments too long, avoid sensible risks and overvalue what we already own. Notice when fear of a small loss is stopping you from a likely gain.'},{topic:'psychology',title:'The planning fallacy',body:'We consistently underestimate how long projects take, even when we know our past projects ran late. The fix is the "outside view": instead of imagining how your project will go, look at how similar projects actually went for others, then adjust from there.'}]},{source:{id:'psychology-of-money',title:'The Psychology of Money',author:'Morgan Housel',type:'book',year:2020,topics:['money','psychology'],emoji:'\ud83d\udcb8',description:'Doing well with money is less about what you know and more about how you behave.'},ideas:[{topic:'money',title:'Behavior beats intelligence',body:'Financial success is less about how smart you are and more about how you behave. A janitor who saves steadily for decades can end up wealthier than a brilliant doctor who overspends. Money is a soft skill, and patience and humility matter more than spreadsheets.'},{topic:'money',title:'Wealth is what you don\'t see',body:'The car someone drives tells you what they spent, not what they have. Real wealth is the money not spent: the investments, the savings, the options. Being rich is a current income; being wealthy is hidden, and it is what gives you freedom later.'},{topic:'money',title:'Compounding needs time',body:'Most of Warren Buffett\'s fortune was made after his 65th birthday. His skill matters, but the real secret is that he started as a child and never stopped. Compounding rewards whoever can stay in the game the longest, so avoid anything that would force you to quit.'},{topic:'money',title:'Enough',body:'The hardest financial skill is getting the goalpost to stop moving. If expectations rise with results, there is never satisfaction. Knowing what "enough" is protects you from risking what you have and need for what you don\'t have and don\'t need.'},{topic:'mindfulness',title:'Money buys control of your time',body:'The highest dividend money pays is the ability to wake up and say "I can do whatever I want today." Controlling your time is one of the strongest predictors of happiness. Savings are not just for emergencies; they buy you options and calm.'}]},{source:{id:'meditations',title:'Meditations',author:'Marcus Aurelius',type:'book',year:180,topics:['philosophy','mindfulness'],emoji:'\ud83c\udfdb\ufe0f',description:'The private journal of a Roman emperor practicing Stoic philosophy.'},ideas:[{topic:'philosophy',title:'Control what you can',body:'Some things are up to us: our judgments, choices and actions. Most things are not: other people, the weather, the past. Stoics put their energy only into the first group. Worrying about the second gives it power over you without changing anything.'},{topic:'philosophy',title:'It\'s your judgment that hurts',body:'Marcus wrote that if you are distressed by something external, the pain is not due to the thing itself but to your estimate of it, and you can revoke that estimate. An insult only harms you if you accept that it does. Between event and reaction, there is a choice.'},{topic:'philosophy',title:'Prepare for difficult people',body:'Each morning Marcus reminded himself he would meet people who were ungrateful, arrogant or dishonest. Not to become cynical, but so he would not be surprised. They act from ignorance of what is good, and he could not be harmed by them unless he chose to be.'},{topic:'mindfulness',title:'Memento mori',body:'Remember that you will die. Marcus returned to this thought often, not out of gloom but for focus. You could leave life right now; let that determine what you do, say and think. Petty grudges and distractions shrink fast next to that fact.'},{topic:'philosophy',title:'The obstacle is the way',body:'"The impediment to action advances action. What stands in the way becomes the way." Every obstacle is a chance to practice patience, courage or creativity. You can\'t always choose your circumstances, but you can always use them as training.'}]},{source:{id:'mans-search-for-meaning',title:"Man's Search for Meaning",author:'Viktor E. Frankl',type:'book',year:1946,topics:['philosophy','psychology'],emoji:'\ud83d\udd6f\ufe0f',description:'A psychiatrist who survived Auschwitz on finding purpose in any circumstance.'},ideas:[{topic:'philosophy',title:'The last human freedom',body:'In the camps, Frankl saw that everything can be taken from a person but one thing: the freedom to choose one\'s attitude in any given set of circumstances. Even when we cannot change a situation, we are challenged to change ourselves.'},{topic:'psychology',title:'A "why" makes the "how" bearable',body:'Frankl noticed that the prisoners most likely to survive often had something to live for: a person waiting for them, or work left to finish. Echoing Nietzsche, he argued that those who have a why to live can bear almost any how.'},{topic:'philosophy',title:'Three sources of meaning',body:'Frankl found meaning comes from three places: creating a work or doing a deed, experiencing something or loving someone, and the attitude we take toward unavoidable suffering. Meaning is not invented in the abstract; it is found in what life asks of us.'},{topic:'career',title:'Success follows, it can\'t be chased',body:'Frankl advised not to aim at success: the more you make it a target, the more you will miss it. Like happiness, it must ensue as the side effect of dedication to a cause greater than yourself, or of love for another person.'}]},{source:{id:'deep-work',title:'Deep Work',author:'Cal Newport',type:'book',year:2016,topics:['productivity','career'],emoji:'\ud83c\udfaf',description:'Rules for focused success in a distracted world.'},ideas:[{topic:'productivity',title:'Deep vs shallow work',body:'Deep work is focused, distraction-free effort that pushes your abilities to their limit and creates real value. Shallow work is email, meetings and admin: easy to copy and low in value. Deep work is becoming rarer exactly as it becomes more valuable.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'Attention residue',body:'When you switch from task A to task B, part of your attention stays stuck on A. Even a quick inbox check leaves residue that lowers your performance on the next task. Batch shallow work and protect long, single-task blocks.'},{topic:'career',title:'High-quality work formula',body:'Newport sums it up as: high-quality work produced = time spent \xd7 intensity of focus. One undistracted hour can beat three fragmented ones. If you want better output, increasing intensity is often easier than adding hours.'},{topic:'mindfulness',title:'Embrace boredom',body:'If you reach for your phone every time you are bored, your brain gets rewired to need constant novelty, and deep focus becomes hard. Practice being bored: wait in line without a screen. Concentration is a skill you train, not a switch you flip.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'Have a shutdown ritual',body:'End each workday with a set routine: check tasks, plan tomorrow, then say a phrase like "shutdown complete". This tells your brain that open loops are captured, so it can truly rest. Recovery in the evening makes deep work possible the next morning.'}]}]},1378,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOKS_2",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{source:{id:'sapiens',title:'Sapiens',author:'Yuval Noah Harari',type:'book',year:2011,topics:['world'],emoji:'\ud83e\uddb4',description:'A brief history of humankind, from foragers to the modern world.'},ideas:[{topic:'world',title:'The power of shared stories',body:'Harari argues that humans came to dominate because we can cooperate flexibly in huge numbers with strangers. We do it by believing in shared stories: nations, money, laws and companies. None exist physically, yet they let millions of people work together.'},{topic:'world',title:'The agricultural trade-off',body:'Farming let populations explode, but Harari suggests many early farmers lived harder lives than foragers: longer hours, narrower diets and more disease. Progress for the species is not always progress for the individual, a pattern worth noticing today.'},{topic:'money',title:'Money is trust',body:'Money is one of the most universal systems of mutual trust ever invented. A coin or a number on a screen has value only because everyone believes everyone else will accept it. It lets total strangers trade without knowing anything about each other.'},{topic:'psychology',title:'Luxuries become necessities',body:'Each new comfort tends to become an expectation, then a need. Email was supposed to save time; instead we now answer dozens of messages a day. Before adopting the next convenience, ask what obligations it might quietly create.'}]},{source:{id:'factfulness',title:'Factfulness',author:'Hans Rosling',type:'book',year:2018,topics:['world','psychology'],emoji:'\ud83d\udcca',description:'Ten reasons we\'re wrong about the world, and why things are better than you think.'},ideas:[{topic:'world',title:'Bad and better at the same time',body:'Things can be bad and improving at once. Child mortality is still tragic, yet it has fallen dramatically over the last two centuries. Thinking only "bad" breeds hopelessness; thinking only "better" breeds complacency. Hold both.'},{topic:'world',title:'The gap instinct',body:'We love splitting the world into two groups: rich and poor, developed and developing. Rosling showed most people actually live in the middle. Data grouped into four income levels gives a much truer picture than "us and them".'},{topic:'psychology',title:'The negativity instinct',body:'News reports what goes wrong, not slow improvements. A plane crash is news; thousands of safe flights are not. So we feel the world is getting worse even when key measures improve. Ask: would I have heard about it if things had gotten better?'},{topic:'psychology',title:'Beware of single numbers',body:'A lonely number can look huge or tiny with no context. Always compare it: to last year, to other countries, per person. Rosling\'s rule: never accept a big number on its own; divide it and compare it before drawing any conclusion.'}]},{source:{id:'how-to-win-friends',title:'How to Win Friends and Influence People',author:'Dale Carnegie',type:'book',year:1936,topics:['relationships','career'],emoji:'\ud83e\udd1d',description:'The classic guide to getting along with people.'},ideas:[{topic:'relationships',title:'Don\'t criticize, condemn or complain',body:'Criticism puts people on the defensive and makes them justify themselves. It wounds pride and breeds resentment, rarely change. Carnegie suggests trying to understand why people do what they do. It is far more useful, and it breeds sympathy and kindness.'},{topic:'relationships',title:'Become genuinely interested in others',body:'You can make more friends in two months by being interested in other people than in two years trying to get them interested in you. Ask questions, remember details, and let people talk about themselves. Everyone\'s favorite topic is their own life.'},{topic:'relationships',title:'A name is the sweetest sound',body:'Remembering and using someone\'s name is a simple, powerful form of respect. Forgetting it signals they did not matter to you. Repeat a new name when you hear it, and use it in conversation. It makes people feel seen.'},{topic:'career',title:'The only way to win an argument',body:'Carnegie concluded the only way to get the best of an argument is to avoid it. Even if you win, the other person feels inferior and resents it. Look for areas of agreement, admit when you are wrong, and let them save face.'}]},{source:{id:'mindset',title:'Mindset',author:'Carol S. Dweck',type:'book',year:2006,topics:['psychology','career'],emoji:'\ud83c\udf31',description:'How believing you can grow changes what you achieve.'},ideas:[{topic:'psychology',title:'Fixed vs growth mindset',body:'With a fixed mindset, you believe talent and intelligence are set at birth, so every challenge is a test you might fail. With a growth mindset, you believe abilities develop through effort and strategy, so challenges are chances to improve.'},{topic:'psychology',title:'The power of "yet"',body:'Adding one word changes the story. "I can\'t do this" becomes "I can\'t do this yet." It turns a verdict into a process. Dweck saw that students who believed they were on a learning curve kept going where others gave up.'},{topic:'relationships',title:'Praise the process, not the person',body:'Praising kids for being "smart" can make them avoid hard tasks, to protect that label. Praising effort, strategies and progress encourages them to take on challenges. The same applies to praising colleagues, friends and yourself.'},{topic:'career',title:'Failure as information',body:'In a growth mindset, failure is not a statement about who you are; it is information about what to try next. Ask "What can I learn from this?" instead of "What does this say about me?" The second question keeps you stuck.'}]},{source:{id:'seven-habits',title:'The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People',author:'Stephen R. Covey',type:'book',year:1989,topics:['productivity','relationships'],emoji:'\ud83e\udded',description:'Principle-centered habits for personal and professional effectiveness.'},ideas:[{topic:'productivity',title:'Be proactive',body:'Between stimulus and response lies our freedom to choose. Reactive people blame circumstances; proactive people focus on what they can influence. Notice your language: "I have to" and "they made me" give your power away. Try "I choose to" instead.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'Begin with the end in mind',body:'Picture your own 80th birthday. What would you want the people you love to say about you? That picture is your real definition of success. Use it as a compass for daily choices, so you don\'t climb a ladder leaning against the wrong wall.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'Important vs urgent',body:'Most people live in urgent tasks: emails, crises, interruptions. Effective people invest in "important but not urgent" work: planning, health, relationships, learning. These rarely shout for attention, which is exactly why they get neglected.'},{topic:'relationships',title:'Seek first to understand',body:'Most people listen with the intent to reply, not to understand. Covey calls for empathic listening: reflect back what the other person feels and means before giving your view. Once people feel understood, they become open to understanding you.'}]},{source:{id:'essentialism',title:'Essentialism',author:'Greg McKeown',type:'book',year:2014,topics:['productivity','mindfulness'],emoji:'\u2702\ufe0f',description:'The disciplined pursuit of less, but better.'},ideas:[{topic:'productivity',title:'Less, but better',body:'Essentialism is not about getting more done; it is about getting the right things done. When you try to do everything, you make a millimeter of progress in a million directions. Choose the few things that matter most and give them your full energy.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'If it isn\'t a clear yes, it\'s a no',body:'When deciding on an opportunity, rate it from 0 to 100. If it is not at least a 90, treat it as a zero and say no. Being selective feels hard, but it frees you for the rare things that would be a "hell yes".'},{topic:'career',title:'Saying no gracefully',body:'Every yes is a no to something else. You can decline without guilt: "I\'d love to, but I\'m over-committed", or "Let me check my priorities and get back to you." People respect clear boundaries more than half-hearted commitments.'},{topic:'mindfulness',title:'Protect the asset',body:'Your best asset for contributing to the world is yourself. Sleep, play and rest are not luxuries; they are what make high performance possible. Skipping sleep to do more is like a pilot skipping fuel to save time.'}]}]},1379,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOKS_3",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{source:{id:'four-thousand-weeks',title:'Four Thousand Weeks',author:'Oliver Burkeman',type:'book',year:2021,topics:['mindfulness','productivity'],emoji:'\u23f3',description:'Time management for mortals: a life is only about 4,000 weeks long.'},ideas:[{topic:'mindfulness',title:'You only get about 4,000 weeks',body:'If you live to 80, you get roughly four thousand weeks. That is shockingly few. Burkeman argues this isn\'t a reason to panic and optimize every minute, but to accept that you will never do everything, and to choose what truly deserves your limited time.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'The efficiency trap',body:'The faster you clear your inbox, the more email you get. Getting more efficient often just creates more to do. Instead of trying to get on top of everything, accept that you never will, and decide consciously what you will neglect.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'Pay yourself first, with time',body:'If you wait until you have free time to do what matters, it will never happen. Take a slice of time for your most important project before the day fills up, even just an hour in the morning. What gets done first gets done.'},{topic:'mindfulness',title:'Limit work in progress',body:'Pick no more than three projects to work on at once. Only when one is done does another get in. Having too many open threads means none of them move. Finishing things, even small ones, beats endlessly starting.'}]},{source:{id:'courage-to-be-disliked',title:'The Courage to Be Disliked',author:'Ichiro Kishimi & Fumitake Koga',type:'book',year:2013,topics:['psychology','relationships'],emoji:'\ud83e\udd8b',description:'Adlerian psychology told as a dialogue between a philosopher and a young man.'},ideas:[{topic:'psychology',title:'Separate your tasks',body:'Ask: whose task is this? How others judge you is their task, not yours. Whether your friend takes your advice is their task. Many problems in relationships come from intruding on other people\'s tasks, or letting them intrude on yours.'},{topic:'psychology',title:'Freedom means being disliked',body:'If you try to be liked by everyone, you end up living by other people\'s expectations. Real freedom means accepting that some people will dislike you. That is the price of living according to your own choices, and it is worth paying.'},{topic:'philosophy',title:'Your past doesn\'t decide your future',body:'Adler rejected the idea that past trauma determines who we are. What matters is the meaning we give our experiences now. We are not driven by the past; we can choose new goals at any moment. The question is not "why am I like this" but "what now?"'},{topic:'relationships',title:'All problems are interpersonal',body:'Adler claimed that nearly all our worries are about relationships. Even loneliness needs other people to exist. The cure is "community feeling": seeing others as comrades rather than competitors, and contributing to them without needing praise.'}]},{source:{id:'steal-like-an-artist',title:'Steal Like an Artist',author:'Austin Kleon',type:'book',year:2012,topics:['creativity'],emoji:'\ud83c\udfa8',description:'10 things nobody told you about being creative.'},ideas:[{topic:'creativity',title:'Nothing is original',body:'Every new idea is a remix of older ones. Artists collect what inspires them and combine it in new ways. Don\'t wait to have a perfectly original idea. Study your heroes, copy to learn, and your own voice emerges from your mix of influences.'},{topic:'creativity',title:'Start before you know who you are',body:'If you wait until you feel ready, you will never start. Make things to discover who you are. Pretend to be the creator you want to be until it becomes true. You figure out your style by doing the work, not by thinking about it.'},{topic:'creativity',title:'Use your hands',body:'Screens make us passive. Kleon keeps two desks: one analog, for sketching and writing by hand, and one digital, for editing and publishing. Getting physical with your ideas often unlocks thinking that a keyboard can\'t.'},{topic:'career',title:'Share your work',body:'Don\'t hoard your ideas. Share what you are learning and making, even when it is unfinished. The internet rewards people who give away their knowledge generously. You don\'t need to be a genius; you need to be part of a scene.'}]},{source:{id:'almanack-naval',title:'The Almanack of Naval Ravikant',author:'Eric Jorgenson',type:'book',year:2020,topics:['money','career','philosophy'],emoji:'\u26f5',description:'Naval Ravikant\'s ideas on wealth and happiness, collected from his talks and posts.'},ideas:[{topic:'money',title:'Seek wealth, not money or status',body:'Naval distinguishes wealth (assets that earn while you sleep) from money (how we transfer wealth) and status (your rank in a social hierarchy). Status games are zero-sum. Wealth creation is positive-sum: everyone can win.'},{topic:'career',title:'Specific knowledge',body:'Build specific knowledge: skills that can\'t be easily trained, because they come from your genuine curiosity and unique path. If society can train someone to do it, it can replace you. Follow what feels like play to you but looks like work to others.'},{topic:'career',title:'Leverage',body:'Fortunes require leverage. The old kinds are labor (people working for you) and capital (money). The new kind is code and media: products with no marginal cost of copying. A video or app can work for you while you sleep.'},{topic:'philosophy',title:'Happiness is a skill',body:'Naval sees happiness as a default state you return to when you remove the sense that something is missing. Desire is a contract you make to be unhappy until you get what you want. Choose your desires carefully, and few of them.'},{topic:'money',title:'Play long-term games',body:'All the returns in life, whether in wealth, relationships or knowledge, come from compound interest. Play long-term games with long-term people. Trust builds over time, and working with the same good people for years is enormously valuable.'}]},{source:{id:'outlive',title:'Outlive',author:'Peter Attia, MD',type:'book',year:2023,topics:['health'],emoji:'\ud83e\udec0',description:'The science and art of longevity: living longer and living better.'},ideas:[{topic:'health',title:'Healthspan over lifespan',body:'Living longer is not enough. Attia focuses on healthspan: the years you live in good physical, mental and emotional health. The goal is to stay strong and sharp into your final decade, not to spend extra years in decline.'},{topic:'health',title:'Train for your "Centenarian Decathlon"',body:'List what you want to be able to do at 90: carry groceries, lift a grandchild, climb stairs, get up off the floor. Then train backward. Because strength and fitness decline with age, you need to build a big reserve now.'},{topic:'health',title:'Zone 2 cardio',body:'Zone 2 is steady exercise where you can still talk, but with effort: brisk cycling, jogging or uphill walking. Attia recommends around three hours per week. It builds the mitochondrial base that supports endurance, metabolism and healthy aging.'},{topic:'health',title:'Strength is protection',body:'Muscle mass and grip strength are strongly linked to longer life. Strength training protects bones, balance and metabolism, and helps prevent the falls that become dangerous later in life. It is one of the best investments in your future self.'}]},{source:{id:'why-we-sleep',title:'Why We Sleep',author:'Matthew Walker',type:'book',year:2017,topics:['health'],emoji:'\ud83d\ude34',description:'A neuroscientist on the power of sleep and dreams.'},ideas:[{topic:'health',title:'Sleep is not optional',body:'Most adults need 7 to 9 hours of sleep. Sleep is when the brain consolidates memories, clears waste and resets emotional balance. Regularly sleeping too little is linked to poorer focus, mood and health. Nobody really "gets used to" sleep deprivation.'},{topic:'health',title:'Keep a regular schedule',body:'Going to bed and waking at the same time every day, even weekends, is one of the most effective sleep habits. Your body clock loves regularity. A big weekend shift is like flying across time zones every Friday and back every Monday.'},{topic:'health',title:'Cool, dark and quiet',body:'Your body needs its core temperature to drop to fall asleep. A cool bedroom, around 18\xb0C (65\xb0F), helps. Dim the lights an hour before bed and keep screens out of the bedroom. Darkness signals the brain to release melatonin.'},{topic:'health',title:'Caffeine lingers',body:'Caffeine has a half-life of around five to six hours. A coffee at 4 pm means a good share of that caffeine is still in your system at bedtime. Even if you can fall asleep, it can reduce deep sleep. Try a caffeine cutoff after lunch.'}]}]},1380,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOKS_4",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{source:{id:'letters-from-a-stoic',title:'Letters from a Stoic',author:'Seneca',type:'book',year:65,topics:['philosophy','mindfulness'],emoji:'\ud83d\udcdc',description:'A Roman statesman\'s letters to a friend on living wisely.'},ideas:[{topic:'philosophy',title:'Hold every hour in your grasp',body:'Seneca opens his letters with a warning: most of our time is taken from us, stolen, or simply slips away. We guard our money closely but give our time to anyone who asks. Treat each hour as something you own, and you will depend less on tomorrow.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'Everywhere means nowhere',body:'Seneca advised reading a few great authors deeply instead of skimming many. Someone who is always traveling has many acquaintances but no friends. Ideas work the same way: food that passes straight through the body doesn\'t nourish it. Choose, digest, return.'},{topic:'philosophy',title:'Practice poverty',body:'Set aside a few days to live on the plainest food and roughest clothes, and ask yourself: "Is this what I feared?" Seneca found that rehearsing hardship in good times takes away its power. Fear of losing comfort often hurts more than the loss itself.'},{topic:'relationships',title:'Choose friends slowly, then trust fully',body:'Think long about whether to admit someone as a friend. But once you decide, welcome them with your whole heart and speak as openly with them as with yourself. Seneca thought it equally wrong to trust everyone and to trust no one.'}]},{source:{id:'enchiridion',title:'The Enchiridion',author:'Epictetus',type:'book',year:125,topics:['philosophy'],emoji:'\u2696\ufe0f',description:'A short Stoic handbook from a philosopher who was born a slave.'},ideas:[{topic:'philosophy',title:'"I returned it", not "I lost it"',body:'Epictetus taught: never say about anything "I have lost it," but "I have returned it." Possessions, status, even people we love are on loan to us. Caring for them as a traveler cares for an inn lets you enjoy them fully without being crushed when they go.'},{topic:'philosophy',title:'Behave like at a banquet',body:'When a dish is passed to you, take a modest portion. If it passes you by, don\'t grab at it. If it hasn\'t reached you yet, don\'t crave it from across the table. Epictetus applied this to money, status and relationships: take what comes, gracefully.'},{topic:'psychology',title:'Be willing to look foolish',body:'If you want to improve, Epictetus said, be content to be thought foolish and ignorant about outside things. Trying to look impressive and trying to grow pull in opposite directions. Learning anything new means looking clumsy for a while.'},{topic:'philosophy',title:'Play your role well',body:'Epictetus compared life to a play: you don\'t choose whether you are cast as a beggar, a ruler or an ordinary citizen. Your job is to play the part you were given as well as you can. Choosing the role is someone else\'s business; performing it is yours.'}]},{source:{id:'tao-te-ching',title:'Tao Te Ching',author:'Lao Tzu',type:'book',topics:['philosophy','mindfulness'],emoji:'\u262f\ufe0f',description:'Ancient Chinese verses on simplicity, humility and going with the flow.'},ideas:[{topic:'philosophy',title:'Be like water',body:'Nothing in the world is softer than water, yet nothing is better at wearing down the hard and strong. Water doesn\'t fight; it flows around obstacles and settles in low places others avoid. The Tao Te Ching sees gentleness and patience as real strength.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'A journey of a thousand miles',body:'The famous line says a journey of a thousand miles begins with a single step; a more literal reading is "beneath your feet". A giant tree grows from a tiny shoot, a tall tower from a pile of earth. Big things are built by handling them while they are still small.'},{topic:'psychology',title:'Knowing yourself is wisdom',body:'Knowing others is intelligence; knowing yourself is true wisdom. Mastering others takes force; mastering yourself takes real strength. Lao Tzu points inward: most of the difficult work in life is understanding and governing your own mind.'},{topic:'money',title:'Enough is rich',body:'The Tao Te Ching says the one who knows he has enough is rich. There is no greater misfortune than not knowing contentment, and no greater fault than always wanting more. Wealth that is never enough can never feel like wealth.'}]},{source:{id:'myth-of-sisyphus',title:'The Myth of Sisyphus',author:'Albert Camus',type:'book',year:1942,topics:['philosophy'],emoji:'\ud83e\udea8',description:'An essay on finding meaning and joy in a universe that offers no answers.'},ideas:[{topic:'philosophy',title:'The absurd',body:'Camus described the absurd as the clash between our hunger for meaning and a universe that stays silent. We ask "why?" and get no answer. He didn\'t think this was a reason for despair, but the honest starting point for living.'},{topic:'philosophy',title:'One must imagine Sisyphus happy',body:'Sisyphus was condemned to roll a boulder uphill forever, only for it to roll back down. Camus argued he could still be happy: the struggle itself toward the heights is enough to fill a heart. Meaning lives in how we push the rock, not in finishing.'},{topic:'philosophy',title:'Revolt by living fully',body:'Camus\'s answer to a meaningless universe is revolt: refusing to give up, and living with as much awareness, passion and freedom as possible. Instead of waiting for an ultimate purpose, he urged us to throw ourselves fully into the life we actually have.'},{topic:'mindfulness',title:'Stop living for later',body:'Camus noticed how often we live for tomorrow: the next promotion, the weekend, someday. Once you accept there is no guaranteed reward at the end, today stops being a waiting room. The present becomes the place where life actually happens.'}]},{source:{id:'power-of-now',title:'The Power of Now',author:'Eckhart Tolle',type:'book',year:1997,topics:['mindfulness'],emoji:'\ud83c\udf05',description:'A guide to escaping compulsive thinking and living in the present.'},ideas:[{topic:'mindfulness',title:'You are not your thoughts',body:'Tolle suggests "watching the thinker": noticing your thoughts as if you were an observer. The moment you notice a thought, a gap opens between you and it. You realize there is an awareness behind the mental chatter, and the chatter loses some of its grip.'},{topic:'mindfulness',title:'Now is all you ever have',body:'The past exists only as memory and the future only as imagination; both are experienced in the present. Yet most worry lives in "what was" and "what if". Bringing attention back to this moment, the only place life happens, is often enough to ease anxiety.'},{topic:'psychology',title:'Accept, then act',body:'Resisting what already is creates suffering on top of pain. Tolle\'s idea of acceptance isn\'t giving up: you first say "yes" to the situation as it is right now, then take action from a clear mind instead of from frustration and fear.'},{topic:'mindfulness',title:'Turn waiting into presence',body:'Waiting in line, in traffic or for a reply can become a practice instead of a frustration. Feel your breath, notice your body, look at what\'s around you. Moments we usually try to skip through are part of life too.'}]},{source:{id:'wherever-you-go',title:'Wherever You Go, There You Are',author:'Jon Kabat-Zinn',type:'book',year:1994,topics:['mindfulness','health'],emoji:'\ud83c\udf0a',description:'Simple mindfulness practice for everyday life, from the creator of MBSR.'},ideas:[{topic:'mindfulness',title:'What mindfulness actually is',body:'Kabat-Zinn defines mindfulness as paying attention in a particular way: on purpose, in the present moment, and non-judgmentally. It isn\'t about emptying your mind or feeling calm. It is simply noticing what is happening, as it happens, without labeling it good or bad.'},{topic:'mindfulness',title:'You can\'t stop the waves',body:'You can\'t stop the waves, but you can learn to surf. Stress, worry and difficult emotions will keep coming. Mindfulness doesn\'t make them disappear; it helps you ride them without being knocked over each time.'},{topic:'psychology',title:'You bring yourself along',body:'The title is the lesson: wherever you go, there you are. A new job, city or relationship won\'t fix what you carry inside. That\'s not bad news; it means the most important change is available right now, wherever you are.'},{topic:'mindfulness',title:'Practice non-doing',body:'We are "human doings" more than human beings. Kabat-Zinn suggests setting aside moments where you do nothing at all: just sit, breathe and let things be. Even five minutes of stillness a day can change how the rest of the day feels.'}]},{source:{id:'miracle-of-mindfulness',title:'The Miracle of Mindfulness',author:'Thich Nhat Hanh',type:'book',year:1975,topics:['mindfulness'],emoji:'\ud83c\udf75',description:'A Zen master\'s gentle guide to bringing awareness into daily life.'},ideas:[{topic:'mindfulness',title:'Wash the dishes to wash the dishes',body:'If you wash dishes while thinking of the tea waiting afterward, you\'re not really alive while washing. And when you drink the tea, you\'ll be thinking of something else. Thich Nhat Hanh\'s advice: do each thing just to do it, fully present.'},{topic:'mindfulness',title:'Your breath is an anchor',body:'Breathing in, know you are breathing in. Breathing out, know you are breathing out. Conscious breathing is the bridge between body and mind. Whenever you feel scattered, a few mindful breaths bring you back home to the present.'},{topic:'mindfulness',title:'Keep a day of mindfulness',body:'Thich Nhat Hanh suggested setting aside one day a week to do everything slowly and with full attention: waking, cleaning, eating, walking. It\'s a reset that makes it easier to stay mindful on the busy days that follow.'},{topic:'health',title:'Walk as if kissing the earth',body:'In walking meditation, you move slowly and feel each step: heel, sole, toes. You arrive with every step, rather than rushing to get somewhere. Even a short mindful walk between meetings can calm a racing mind.'}]},{source:{id:'influence',title:'Influence',author:'Robert B. Cialdini',type:'book',year:1984,topics:['psychology','career'],emoji:'\ud83e\uddf2',description:'The psychology of persuasion, and how to spot it being used on you.'},ideas:[{topic:'psychology',title:'Reciprocity',body:'We feel a strong urge to repay what others give us, even small, unrequested gifts. That\'s why free samples and "complimentary" mints with the bill work so well. Be generous for real, and be aware when a gift is designed to create obligation.'},{topic:'psychology',title:'Social proof',body:'When unsure, we look at what others are doing and copy them. "Best-seller", "most popular" and long queues all signal "this must be good". It\'s a useful shortcut, but crowds can be wrong, especially when everyone is copying everyone else.'},{topic:'money',title:'Scarcity',body:'Things seem more valuable when they\'re rare or about to disappear: "only 2 left", "offer ends tonight". When you feel that rush to act fast, pause and ask: would I want this just as much if there were plenty of it?'},{topic:'psychology',title:'Commitment and consistency',body:'Once we take a small stand, we feel pressure to act consistently with it. Agreeing to a tiny request makes us more likely to agree to a bigger one later. Use it for good: commit publicly to small steps toward your own goals.'}]},{source:{id:'flow',title:'Flow',author:'Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi',type:'book',year:1990,topics:['psychology','productivity'],emoji:'\ud83c\udf00',description:'The psychology of optimal experience and deep enjoyment.'},ideas:[{topic:'psychology',title:'What flow feels like',body:'Flow is the state of being so absorbed in an activity that nothing else seems to matter. Time distorts, self-consciousness fades, and the activity becomes its own reward. Csikszentmihalyi found people describe their best moments this way, not their most relaxing ones.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'Balance challenge and skill',body:'Flow happens when a challenge stretches your skills just a little. Too hard, and you feel anxious. Too easy, and you get bored. To find flow, keep raising the difficulty as you improve, like a game that gets harder level by level.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'Clear goals, fast feedback',body:'Flow is easier when you know exactly what you\'re trying to do and can tell right away how you\'re doing. Turn vague tasks into concrete ones ("write 300 words", not "work on the essay") and find ways to see your progress as you go.'},{topic:'mindfulness',title:'Happiness is made, not found',body:'Csikszentmihalyi argued that the best moments rarely come from passive leisure. They come when we stretch body or mind to accomplish something difficult and worthwhile. Happiness is less something that happens to us and more something we build through engagement.'}]},{source:{id:'happiness-hypothesis',title:'The Happiness Hypothesis',author:'Jonathan Haidt',type:'book',year:2006,topics:['psychology','philosophy'],emoji:'\ud83d\udc18',description:'Ancient wisdom tested against modern psychology.'},ideas:[{topic:'psychology',title:'The elephant and the rider',body:'Haidt pictures the mind as a small rider (conscious reasoning) on a large elephant (emotions and intuition). The rider can guide, but when they disagree, the elephant usually wins. Lasting change means training the elephant, not just lecturing the rider.'},{topic:'psychology',title:'The hedonic treadmill',body:'We adapt quickly to good fortune. A raise, a new car or a bigger home feels great at first, then becomes normal. Because of this adaptation, chasing more stuff rarely brings lasting happiness. What we adapt to more slowly: relationships, meaningful work, and freedom from long commutes.'},{topic:'relationships',title:'Happiness comes from between',body:'Haidt concludes that happiness doesn\'t come purely from within or from outside circumstances. It comes from between: from the right relationships with people, with work you care about, and with something larger than yourself.'},{topic:'philosophy',title:'Adversity can help you grow',body:'Hardship isn\'t good in itself, but many people report growing from it: discovering hidden strengths, deepening relationships and changing priorities. Researchers call it post-traumatic growth. Making sense of what happened, by talking or writing about it, seems to help.'}]}]},1381,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOKS_5",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{source:{id:'richest-man-in-babylon',title:'The Richest Man in Babylon',author:'George S. Clason',type:'book',year:1926,topics:['money'],emoji:'\ud83c\udffa',description:'Timeless money lessons told as parables from ancient Babylon.'},ideas:[{topic:'money',title:'Pay yourself first',body:'The first lesson of Babylon: a part of all you earn is yours to keep. Before paying anyone else, set aside at least one tenth of your income. You pay the baker, the landlord and the merchant, so why not pay your future self too?'},{topic:'money',title:'Control your expenses',body:'What we call "necessary expenses" tend to grow until they equal our income, unless we decide otherwise. Clason\'s advice: budget so you can pay for what you truly need and enjoy, without spending more than nine tenths of what you earn.'},{topic:'money',title:'Make your gold multiply',body:'Savings are only the start. Every coin you save can become a worker that earns more coins, and each of those can earn more. Put your money to work so that it brings in income, building a stream of wealth that flows even when you aren\'t working.'},{topic:'money',title:'Guard your treasure from loss',body:'The first rule of investing is to protect your principal. Be wary of schemes promising unusually high returns, and seek advice from people experienced in handling money, not from friends with no track record. Small, secure returns beat big, risky losses.'}]},{source:{id:'common-sense-investing',title:'The Little Book of Common Sense Investing',author:'John C. Bogle',type:'book',year:2007,topics:['money'],emoji:'\ud83d\udcc8',description:'The founder of Vanguard on why low-cost index funds win.'},ideas:[{topic:'money',title:"Don't look for the needle, buy the haystack",body:'Instead of trying to pick the few winning stocks, Bogle\'s advice was to own the whole market through a low-cost index fund. You get the market\'s return, minus tiny costs, and you never have to guess which company will win.'},{topic:'money',title:'Costs matter enormously',body:'Fees look small, like 1% or 2% a year, but over decades they compound against you and can eat a large share of your gains. In investing, you get what you don\'t pay for. Lower costs are one of the few things you can control.'},{topic:'money',title:'Most professionals lose to the index',body:'Over long periods, the large majority of actively managed funds do worse than a simple index fund after fees. As a group, investors earn the market return before costs, so after costs, the average active investor must fall behind.'},{topic:'psychology',title:'Stay the course',body:'Bogle\'s motto: stay the course. Trying to time the market by jumping in and out usually means buying high and selling low. Pick a sensible mix of stocks and bonds, keep investing regularly, and ignore the daily noise.'}]},{source:{id:'die-with-zero',title:'Die With Zero',author:'Bill Perkins',type:'book',year:2020,topics:['money','mindfulness'],emoji:'\ud83c\udf92',description:'Getting all you can from your money and your life.'},ideas:[{topic:'money',title:'Money is for living',body:'Perkins argues that many people save too much for too long and die with wealth they never enjoyed. The goal isn\'t to maximize money, but to maximize life experiences. Every dollar saved and never spent represents hours of work you gave away for nothing.'},{topic:'mindfulness',title:'Memory dividends',body:'Experiences keep paying off long after they end. Every time you remember, retell or laugh about a trip or adventure, you collect a "memory dividend". That\'s why experiences earlier in life are especially valuable: you get to enjoy those memories for longer.'},{topic:'philosophy',title:'Some doors close forever',body:'Some experiences only fit certain windows of life: backpacking in your twenties, playing with your kids when they\'re young, hiking while your knees are strong. Perkins suggests mapping your life into "time buckets" and planning experiences before their window closes.'},{topic:'relationships',title:'Give while you\'re alive',body:'An inheritance often arrives when children are in their fifties or sixties, when it helps them least. Perkins suggests giving money to your kids or causes you care about while you\'re alive, when it can make the biggest difference, and you get to see the impact.'}]},{source:{id:'rich-dad-poor-dad',title:'Rich Dad Poor Dad',author:'Robert T. Kiyosaki',type:'book',year:1997,topics:['money','career'],emoji:'\ud83c\udfe0',description:'What the rich teach their kids about money, through the story of two fathers.'},ideas:[{topic:'money',title:'Assets vs liabilities',body:'Kiyosaki\'s simple definition: an asset puts money in your pocket, a liability takes money out. By that test, many things people call assets, like an expensive car, are really liabilities. Focus on buying things that pay you, not things that cost you every month.'},{topic:'money',title:'Escape the rat race',body:'Many people earn more, then spend more, staying stuck in a loop of working to pay bills. Kiyosaki calls this the rat race. The way out is to grow income from investments and businesses until it covers your expenses, so work becomes a choice.'},{topic:'career',title:'Work to learn, not to earn',body:'Early in your career, choose jobs for the skills they teach, not just the paycheck. Kiyosaki recommends learning sales, communication, management and how money works. These skills compound over a lifetime far more than a slightly higher starting salary.'},{topic:'psychology',title:'"How can I afford it?"',body:'Saying "I can\'t afford it" ends the conversation. Asking "How can I afford it?" starts your brain looking for solutions. The point isn\'t to buy everything you want, but to train yourself to think creatively about money rather than shutting the door.'}]},{source:{id:'getting-things-done',title:'Getting Things Done',author:'David Allen',type:'book',year:2001,topics:['productivity'],emoji:'\u2705',description:'The art of stress-free productivity.'},ideas:[{topic:'productivity',title:'Your mind is for having ideas',body:'David Allen says your mind is for having ideas, not holding them. Every unfinished task you try to remember takes up mental space and creates low-level stress. Capture everything (tasks, ideas, errands) in a trusted system outside your head.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'The two-minute rule',body:'When processing your inbox or task list, if something takes less than two minutes, do it right away. Tracking and reviewing it later would take longer than just doing it. Everything bigger gets put on a list or scheduled.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'Define the next action',body:'"Plan vacation" is not a task; it\'s a project. The next action might be "text Sam to ask for dates." Projects stall when they sit on your list as vague blobs. Always ask: what\'s the very next physical action that moves this forward?'},{topic:'productivity',title:'Do a weekly review',body:'Once a week, empty your inboxes, update your lists, and look at your calendar and projects. Allen considers the weekly review the key habit that keeps the whole system trustworthy. Without it, lists go stale and your brain starts holding everything again.'}]},{source:{id:'eat-that-frog',title:'Eat That Frog!',author:'Brian Tracy',type:'book',year:2001,topics:['productivity'],emoji:'\ud83d\udc38',description:'21 ways to stop procrastinating and get more done in less time.'},ideas:[{topic:'productivity',title:'Eat the frog first',body:'If you had to eat a live frog, it would be best to do it first thing in the morning; nothing worse will happen the rest of the day. Your "frog" is your biggest, most important task, the one you\'re most likely to put off. Do it first.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'The ABCDE method',body:'Label each task: A (must do, serious consequences), B (should do), C (nice to do), D (delegate), E (eliminate). Never do a B task while an A task is left undone. This turns a messy to-do list into a clear order of attack.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'Apply the 80/20 rule',body:'Often, about 20% of your tasks produce about 80% of your results. Tracy suggests finding those few high-value tasks and working on them first. Being busy with the other 80% can feel productive while moving you forward very little.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'Plan tomorrow tonight',body:'End each day by writing out tomorrow\'s tasks. Your subconscious works on the list overnight, and you start the next morning knowing exactly what to do instead of deciding while groggy. Tracy claims every minute of planning saves several in execution.'}]},{source:{id:'the-one-thing',title:'The ONE Thing',author:'Gary Keller & Jay Papasan',type:'book',year:2013,topics:['productivity','career'],emoji:'1\ufe0f\u20e3',description:'The surprisingly simple truth behind extraordinary results.'},ideas:[{topic:'productivity',title:'The focusing question',body:'Ask: "What\'s the ONE Thing I can do such that by doing it, everything else will be easier or unnecessary?" Apply it to your day, your career, your health and your relationships. It forces you to find the lead domino instead of spreading yourself thin.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'The domino effect',body:'A single domino can knock over one about 50% larger. Line them up, and small pushes topple surprisingly big blocks. Success works the same way: get the right first thing done, and it makes the next, bigger thing possible.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'Multitasking is a lie',body:'When we "multitask", we are actually switching rapidly between tasks, and each switch costs time and focus. Doing two things at once usually means doing both worse. Give your ONE Thing your undivided attention.'},{topic:'career',title:'Time-block your ONE Thing',body:'Keller and Papasan recommend blocking several hours a day, ideally in the morning, for your most important work, and protecting it like a meeting with your boss. Without a fixed block, urgent small things will always crowd out important big ones.'}]},{source:{id:'indistractable',title:'Indistractable',author:'Nir Eyal',type:'book',year:2019,topics:['productivity','psychology'],emoji:'\ud83d\udcf5',description:'How to control your attention and choose your life.'},ideas:[{topic:'psychology',title:'Distraction starts inside',body:'We blame phones and apps, but Eyal argues most distraction is an escape from discomfort: boredom, stress, uncertainty. The phone is just the nearest exit. To master distraction, notice the feeling that comes right before you reach for it.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'Traction vs distraction',body:'Traction is any action that pulls you toward what you planned. Distraction pulls you away. Watching a show isn\'t a distraction if you scheduled it; checking email is if you planned to write. The difference is intent, not the activity itself.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'Timebox your calendar',body:'You can\'t call something a distraction unless you know what it distracted you from. Eyal recommends planning your week in time blocks, including time for yourself, relationships and work. A to-do list says what to do; a calendar says when.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'Make pacts with yourself',body:'Decide in advance, when you\'re clear-headed. Use effort pacts (apps that block sites), price pacts (put money on the line) and identity pacts ("I\'m someone who doesn\'t check my phone at dinner"). Precommitment protects you from your future, tempted self.'}]}]},1382,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"MEDIA",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{source:{id:'huberman-lab',title:'Huberman Lab',author:'Andrew Huberman',type:'podcast',topics:['health','mindfulness'],emoji:'\ud83c\udfa7',description:'A Stanford neuroscientist on science-based tools for everyday life.'},ideas:[{topic:'health',title:'Get morning light',body:'Viewing daylight outside within an hour or so of waking helps set your body clock. It boosts alertness during the day and helps you feel sleepy at night. Even on cloudy days, outdoor light is far brighter than indoor lighting. Try a 5 to 15 minute walk.'},{topic:'mindfulness',title:'The physiological sigh',body:'To calm down fast, take two inhales through the nose (a deep one, then a short top-up), followed by a long, slow exhale through the mouth. A Stanford study found a few minutes of this "cyclic sighing" a day improved mood and lowered stress.'},{topic:'health',title:'Delay your caffeine',body:'Huberman suggests waiting 60 to 90 minutes after waking before your first coffee, so you rely on your body\'s natural morning alertness first. Some people find it helps avoid an afternoon crash. Experiment and notice how you feel.'},{topic:'productivity',title:'Focus comes in 90-minute blocks',body:'Many people find they can sustain intense focus for roughly 90 minutes before needing a break. The first few minutes of any session feel restless; that is normal. Push through the friction, then rest properly, without your phone, before the next block.'}]},{source:{id:'paul-graham-essays',title:'Essays',author:'Paul Graham',type:'article',topics:['career','creativity'],emoji:'\u270d\ufe0f',description:'Essays on startups, work and thinking by the co-founder of Y Combinator.'},ideas:[{topic:'productivity',title:"Maker's schedule, manager's schedule",body:'Managers work in one-hour slots, so a meeting costs them an hour. Makers (writers, programmers, designers) need half-day blocks, so one meeting in the middle of an afternoon can wreck the whole thing. If you make things, cluster meetings and guard long stretches.'},{topic:'career',title:"Do things that don't scale",body:'Startups often begin by doing things by hand that could never work at large scale: personally recruiting users one by one, or obsessing over each early customer. Graham argues this is how most great companies actually got started.'},{topic:'philosophy',title:'Keep your identity small',body:'Why are discussions about politics and religion so often useless? Because they are part of people\'s identity. The more labels you attach to yourself, the more topics make you defensive and dumb. Let fewer things become part of who you are.'},{topic:'creativity',title:'How to do great work',body:'Graham\'s recipe: choose a field you have a natural aptitude for and a deep interest in, learn enough to reach the frontier of knowledge, notice the gaps, and explore the promising ones. Curiosity, delight and the desire to do something impressive are the fuel.'}]},{source:{id:'tail-end',title:'The Tail End',author:'Tim Urban (Wait But Why)',type:'article',year:2015,topics:['relationships','mindfulness'],emoji:'\ud83d\uddd3\ufe0f',description:'Visualizing a human life in weeks, and what that means for the people we love.'},ideas:[{topic:'mindfulness',title:'Your life in weeks',body:'A 90-year life fits on a single page as a grid of about 4,700 small boxes, one per week. Seeing it this way makes time feel finite and concrete. Each box is a week you get to spend, and you can see how many you have left.'},{topic:'relationships',title:'The tail end with your parents',body:'Tim Urban realized that by the time he finished high school, he had already used up about 93% of the in-person time he would ever spend with his parents. The rest is the tail end. If time with someone matters, it may be scarcer than it feels.'},{topic:'relationships',title:'Prioritize the people',body:'Three takeaways from "The Tail End": live near the people you love, prioritize time with them, and remember that quality time spent together is finite. A visit you keep postponing might be one of a surprisingly small number left.'}]},{source:{id:'our-world-in-data',title:'Our World in Data',author:'Max Roser et al.',type:'website',topics:['world'],emoji:'\ud83c\udf0d',description:'Research and data on the world\'s largest problems.'},ideas:[{topic:'world',title:'Awful, much better, can be better',body:'Max Roser sums up the world in three statements that are all true at once: the world is awful, the world is much better, and the world can be much better. Seeing only one of these leads to despair, complacency or naivety.'},{topic:'world',title:'Child mortality has plummeted',body:'In 1800, roughly 4 in 10 children worldwide died before their fifth birthday. Today it is around 4 in 100. Vaccines, clean water, better nutrition and medicine made this possible. It is one of humanity\'s greatest, and least talked about, achievements.'},{topic:'world',title:'Most of the world can now read',body:'Two centuries ago, only around one in ten adults could read. Today the large majority of adults worldwide are literate. Education has spread faster in the last century than in all of previous history combined, though big gaps remain.'},{topic:'world',title:'Extreme poverty is falling',body:'In the early 1800s, the vast majority of humanity lived in what we would now call extreme poverty. Today it is under one in ten. Hundreds of millions remain poor, and progress has slowed recently, but the long-run trend shows change is possible.'}]},{source:{id:'waldinger-ted',title:'What Makes a Good Life?',author:'Robert Waldinger (TED)',type:'video',year:2015,topics:['relationships','health'],emoji:'\u2764\ufe0f',description:'Lessons from the Harvard Study of Adult Development, one of the longest studies of adult life.'},ideas:[{topic:'relationships',title:'Good relationships keep us healthy',body:'The Harvard Study of Adult Development followed hundreds of men for over 75 years. Its clearest message: good relationships keep us happier and healthier. People most satisfied with their relationships at 50 were the healthiest at 80.'},{topic:'relationships',title:'Loneliness is toxic',body:'People who are more socially connected to family, friends and community tend to be happier, healthier and live longer than people who are less well connected. Feeling lonely is linked to earlier decline in health and brain function.'},{topic:'relationships',title:'Quality over quantity',body:'It is not the number of friends you have, or whether you are in a committed relationship. It is the quality of your close relationships that matters. Living amid constant conflict can be bad for health; warm, dependable relationships are protective.'},{topic:'relationships',title:'Lean into relationships',body:'Replace screen time with people time. Liven up a stale relationship by doing something new together. Reach out to a family member you haven\'t spoken to in years. Relationships take ongoing work, and they pay you back like nothing else.'}]},{source:{id:'brene-brown-ted',title:'The Power of Vulnerability',author:'Bren\xe9 Brown (TED)',type:'video',year:2010,topics:['relationships','psychology'],emoji:'\ud83e\udef6',description:'A researcher on connection, courage and the willingness to be seen.'},ideas:[{topic:'relationships',title:'Connection requires being seen',body:'Bren\xe9 Brown\'s research found that people with a strong sense of love and belonging share one thing: they believe they are worthy of it. To connect, we have to allow ourselves to be truly seen, imperfections included.'},{topic:'psychology',title:'You can\'t numb selectively',body:'When we numb hard feelings like fear, shame and grief, we also numb joy, gratitude and happiness. There is no selective switch. Leaning into discomfort is the price of feeling the good things fully.'},{topic:'psychology',title:'Vulnerability is courage',body:'Vulnerability is often seen as weakness, but it is the birthplace of creativity, love and belonging. Saying "I love you" first, asking for help or trying something new with no guarantees: these take courage, not weakness.'}]},{source:{id:'ferriss-fear-setting',title:'Why You Should Define Your Fears Instead of Your Goals',author:'Tim Ferriss (TED)',type:'video',year:2017,topics:['psychology','career'],emoji:'\ud83e\ude82',description:'A Stoic-inspired exercise for making hard decisions.'},ideas:[{topic:'psychology',title:'Fear-setting',body:'Instead of goal-setting, try fear-setting. Write down the worst things that could happen if you take a scary step. Next to each, note how you could prevent it, and how you could repair the damage if it happened. Fears look smaller on paper.'},{topic:'career',title:'The cost of inaction',body:'We weigh the risks of acting but rarely the cost of doing nothing. Ask: if I avoid this decision, what will my life look like in 6 months, 1 year, 3 years? Inaction has a price too; it is just paid slowly and quietly.'},{topic:'philosophy',title:'Suffer more in imagination',body:'Seneca wrote that we suffer more often in imagination than in reality. Ferriss turns this into practice: name your fears precisely. Vague anxieties feel enormous; specific ones are usually manageable and often surprisingly reversible.'}]},{source:{id:'farnam-street',title:'Mental Models',author:'Farnam Street',type:'website',topics:['psychology','career'],emoji:'\ud83e\udde9',description:'Timeless thinking tools for making better decisions.'},ideas:[{topic:'psychology',title:'First principles thinking',body:'Break a problem down to the basic truths you know for sure, then build up from there, instead of reasoning by analogy ("this is how it\'s always done"). It is harder work, but it is how people find solutions that others miss.'},{topic:'psychology',title:'Second-order thinking',body:'First-order thinking asks "what happens next?" Second-order thinking asks "and then what?" Eating cake feels good now (first order) but works against your health goals (second order). Most people stop at the first step.'},{topic:'psychology',title:'Inversion',body:'Instead of asking how to succeed, ask what would guarantee failure, then avoid it. "How do I have a great relationship?" becomes "What would ruin one?" Avoiding stupidity is often easier than seeking brilliance.'},{topic:'career',title:'Circle of competence',body:'Everyone has areas where they truly know what they are doing. The key is knowing where the edges of that circle are. Inside it, you can act with confidence. Outside it, slow down, ask experts and be honest about what you don\'t know.'},{topic:'psychology',title:'The map is not the territory',body:'Every model, plan or statistic is a simplification of reality. Maps are useful, but they leave things out. When reality doesn\'t match your map, update the map; don\'t argue with the territory.'}]}]},1383,[]);
